Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 8.3. Evaluation of Offers. <omitted> (c) The Company Board, when evaluating an Acquisition Proposal, must consider the proposal in its entirety, including financial, strategic, and legal implications. It is imperative that the Board, in good faith, consults with its outside legal counsel and a financial advisor of internationally recognized reputation. The Board shall also evaluate the potential impact on the Company’s long-term strategic goals and the interests of all stakeholders, not solely the shareholders. The decision must align with the fiduciary duties of the Board under applicable Law. Furthermore, the Board is required to negotiate in good faith with the proposing party to ensure that the terms of the proposal align with the Company’s strategic vision, after which the Board must determine if the proposal remains a Superior Proposal. The Board must provide written notice to the Parent of its intention to change its recommendation not earlier than 11:59 p.m. New York time on the fifth Business Day after Parent receives such notice, during which time negotiations may continue. (Pages 82-83)\nAnswer: 
Choices:

