Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 8.5. Evaluation of Acquisition Proposals. <omitted> (c) During the Pre-Closing Period, the Company Board may alter its recommendation regarding this Agreement or terminate this Agreement to enter into an Alternative Acquisition Agreement if, and only if, <omitted> (ii) the Company Board determines in good faith, after consultation with its outside legal counsel and a financial advisor of nationally recognized reputation, that the failure to make such a change or terminate the Agreement would be inconsistent with the fiduciary duties of the Company Board under applicable Law, (iii) the Company has provided Parent with at least five Business Days' prior written notice of the Company Board's intention to make a change or terminate this Agreement, and (iv) the Company Board has evaluated the strategic alignment of the Acquisition Proposal with the Company's long-term business objectives and compliance with applicable regulatory requirements. The Company Board must also consider the potential impact on the Company's employees and customers. The determination that an Acquisition Proposal is a 'Superior Proposal' must be made after considering all these factors. (Pages 82-83)\nAnswer:
Choices:

