Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 9.4. Evaluation of Proposals. <omitted> (b) During the Evaluation Period, the Company Board may consider an Acquisition Proposal only if: (i) the proposal is in writing and includes all relevant terms, (ii) the Company Board determines, after consultation with its external legal counsel and strategic advisors, that the proposal is bona fide and potentially superior, (iii) the Company Board considers the strategic alignment and potential synergies in addition to the financial terms, and (iv) the Company Board determines in good faith, after reviewing the results of negotiations with the proposing party and consulting with a financial advisor of national reputation, that the proposal would likely result in a transaction more favorable than the current agreement, considering legal, fiduciary, and financial responsibilities. Furthermore, any changes to the financial terms or other material aspects of the proposal shall require a new evaluation period. (Pages 82-83)\nAnswer:
